c exam which is  the test of proficiency in korean i may  just do that  but i don't know yet so  the some people have said well is this  like the plus one challenge  that  i think his name is brian kwong is doing  or is this like benny with his three  months to fluency  and i think there are other challenges  out there  and of course there are similarities  because it's all about learning  languages  uh three months or 90 days is a handy  period of time  um however uh i i i must admit i haven't  i haven't really looked at very  carefully  what benny does in his 30 days or in his  three months rather what brian kwong's  plus one consists of i had a a brief  look there are a bunch of questions  about goals and rewards and  and punishments or something and this  kind of  here's what we're doing okay  all i really want to do is step up my  level of activity  uh i'm not promising myself any specific  results  and i'm inviting other people to do the  same and  just by being more active with the  language being a little more disciplined  developing some habits  um  we inevitably learn more  and i'm going to try to be more  disciplined for example i'm looking for  a  an app that will track  my use of time because i say i study an  hour a day i think i probably study more  than an hour a day because typically  i'll read a bit in the morning if i'm in  the car  it could be 45 minutes to an hour then i  wash the dishes all the time i'm  listening and then i might do some  linking at night so probably closer to  two hours i want to find out exactly how  much time i spend and i think that will  be an inducement to be more systematic  in spending that time  uh  so those are the kinds of things that  i'm going to be doing and i'm going to  be sharing this and you know resources  for for korean i gave up on korean  because there were no interesting  resources a lot of resources have been  added to our library at link in korean  i'm going to use those but i'm also  going to look for others i may end up  asking people to record stuff for me and  i can share that with you so  but other people will be doing other  languages and they may be starting from  scratch or like me they may be  you know sort of stagnant in a level in  a language and they want to make a  breakthrough so the motives could be  will be varied the amount of time that  people have to spend will be varied  uh  but  you know it's not about speaking from  day one  if anything as you know i am more of an  input based learner link is structured  around the idea that the most efficient  way of learning a language is to expose  yourself to the language to meaningful  content that's  not too difficult for you and to acquire  vocabulary and to let your brain become  familiar with the language and the more  intensively you do this and this is what  happened to me when i was learning  mandarin i did so much listening and  reading that i ended up learning faster  than the others who may have been more  into  speaking finding other learners to talk  to  which  for people who like to do that that's  fine but i don't believe in that and i  think even when you are  say  speaking early  most of the benefit there is that you  are listening  and uh it is true and i often refer to  sort of the resonance of different  language learning situations and a real  situation face to face is higher  resonance than listening to an mp3  player however  if you have a very limited vocabulary  and can't say much then at the end of  half an hour you haven't been exposed to  very much of the language so in that  situation  at an early stage i prefer to listen and  read because it's a  richer language environment and then  when i am able to defend myself a little  better then i can have more meaningful  conversation so with regard to my korean  for example i'll be focusing on a lot of  listening and reading to get my  comprehension skills up improve my  vocabulary and then eventually i'll be  stepping up the speaking because the  speaking is more real it's higher  resonance i mentioned for example i was  in  czech republic in slovakia i was with my  wife and my son and his family so i  couldn't speak that much czech  all day long but just being in the real  environment uh people are speaking the  language in stores in little  interactions that you have with people  then newspaper and so forth  uh  there's something about that real  environment that that really makes any  interaction  resonate more  so therefore face-to-face is extremely  important  but there's a trade-off between the sort  of substance  uh the sort of richness of the language  exchange and this realia or the real  nature of the exchange  so i'm gonna be balancing those as i do  my  my korean  one thing i also wanted to say  you know i'm not starting from scratch  but for those of you who are starting  from scratch  in the 90 day challenge just a few bits  of advice for someone starting from  scratch  the big thing as i always repeat this  language learning is attitude time with  the language and learning to notice  so  one of the problems people have with  let's say french let's say they're  starting into french okay right off the  bat you have to notice that there are a  lot of  consonants you know letters in french  that aren't pronounced  that's just what it is there's no point  in reacting negatively to it and saying  this is stupid or you know it's  difficult it just is the x isn't  pronounced and it drags on as an s you  know  is the x isn't pronounced um  but if you say uh okay  or noel  but  i'm trying to think of uh  if it was  or spies  you know as it drags on so they have all  these uh  connecting sounds like  panna in the for example  part 2.  you don't know which one has the s and  which one doesn't  and and you won't be able to really even  if you study it it won't stick you just  have to accept the fact that just  recognize that it's there have a  positive attitude towards it and realize  that with enough exposure over a long  enough period of time and with  sufficiently intense exposure it'll  start to make sense so  to point  and then  and the nt at the end isn't pronounced  unless of course it's followed by vowel  in which case  the t gets pronounced and these are  things that it's like the russian thing  someone told me you know that uh  you know  one of something is in the nominative  singular two three and four is in the  genitive singular and five and more is  in the generally plural it you can't  absorb that kind of information  all you can sort of register is that  these words change  and watch for it so you're you're being  attentive you're trying to notice what's  happening and you're not resisting it so  you've got an open mind you've got a  good attitude and you want to learn it  and you're spending the time  and and if you do that enough and  occasionally review the rules without  trying to nail them down you have to be  confident that you're going to learn  so  i kind of introduced that because the  main thing is the 90-day challenge is to  step up our level of activity and that  is what we measure at link we don't  measure  where you are achievements level you  know  framework of reference  b2 it's how active are you how much are  you reading how much are you listening  how much are you linking in other words  saving words and phrases to your  database and if you step up that level  of activity you will do better i can  guarantee you that it's been my  experience over you know many many years  of learning many languages  step up your level of activity  interact with the language have a  positive and open and enthusiastic  emotionally committed attitude and  notice what's there  without necessarily hoping to nail it  down  and if we can do those things in the  90-day challenge then i think  all the participants are going to  benefit  so just a little further and if you have  any questions on the 90d challenge  please ask me and i can assure you i'm  still working on my russian in czech i'm  not going to start my korean until  january the 15th all right then  join the 90-day challenge  bye for now

